{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a8f24306129247e5","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9017\n3\tepic_count: 2\n4\tcritical_path_length: 2\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\trevised: false\n7\ttriage: null\n8\ttriage_rationale: null\n9\t---\n10\t# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-9017\n11\t\n12\tStrategy: Keyboard accessibility conformance for core Dashboard flows.\n13\t\n14\tTriage note: the strategy was not triaged as below-threshold or docs-only. Although\n15\tthe scope is small, it spans two architecturally distinct work streams that warrant\n16\tseparate PR review cycles — Dashboard code remediation (keyboard operability and\n17\tfocus indicators) and substantive conformance-documentation authoring (VPAT and\n18\taccessibility statement, new/rewritten published content). Per the Step 0 escape\n19\tconditions, both streams were decomposed in full.\n20\t\n21\tThe strategy reports no open questions (\"the audit report enumerates the exact\n22\tdefects per flow\"), so no Investigation epics were created. The Dashboard is\n23\tKonflux-onboarded (per architecture context), so no onboarding-chain epics are\n24\tneeded.\n25\t\n26\t## Epic List\n27\t\n28\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n29\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n30\t| RHAISTRAT-9017-E001 | Keyboard accessibility remediation for the four audited Dashboard flows | Implementation | RHOAI Dashboard | P1 |\n31\t| RHAISTRAT-9017-E002 | Update and republish accessibility conformance documentation (VPAT and accessibility statement) | Implementation (docs-authoring) | RHOAI Documentation | P2 |\n32\t\n33\t## Dependency DAG\n34\t\n35\t```mermaid\n36\tgraph TD\n37\t    E001[\"E001 — Dashboard keyboard accessibility fixes (P1)\"] --> E002[\"E002 — VPAT / accessibility statement update and republish (P2)\"]\n38\t```\n39\t\n40\t## DAG Justification\n41\t\n42\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n43\t|------|------|-----------|\n44\t| E001 → E002 | Rule 11 (docs-authoring blocked by all Implementation epics) | The conformance documentation describes the fixed state; the VPAT claims and the accessibility statement can only be written and technically reviewed against the implemented keyboard-accessibility behavior. E001 is the only other Implementation epic, so it is the sole blocker. |\n45\t\n46\tNo other edges: E001 has no dependencies (the audit enumerates the defects; the\n47\tDashboard repo and build pipeline already exist), and there is exactly one\n48\timplementation work stream in the Dashboard component, so nothing runs in parallel\n49\twith anything else.\n50\t\n51\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n52\t\n53\t| HLR | Priority | Epic | Notes |\n54\t|-----|----------|------|-------|\n55\t| HLR-1 | P1 | E001 | Keyboard operability of the four audited flows. |\n56\t| HLR-2 | P1 | E001 | Consistent visible focus indicators. Covered in the same epic as HLR-1: both HLRs are remediated by the same pass over the same controls and UI surfaces in the same four flows — focus traps, reachability, and focus indication are one keyboard-accessibility change per control, so splitting them would build/audit the same surface twice. Same priority (P1), so no priority split is involved. |\n57\t| HLR-3 | P2 | E002 | VPAT and product-docs accessibility statement update, republish, technical review. |\n58\t\n59\tAll three HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements.\n60\t\n61\t## Health Warnings\n62\t\n63\t1. **Missing strategy sections (non-blocking).** The strategy has no Impacted Teams\n64\t   table, no Dependencies table, no Given/When/Then acceptance criteria, and no\n65\t   Non-Functional Requirements section. Decomposition proceeded with available\n66\t   information. Team assignments were derived: the Dashboard epic's team\n67\t   (RHOAI Dashboard) comes from\n68\t   `.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.0/dashboard.md`; the\n69\t   Documentation epic's team (RHOAI Documentation) is inferred — verify with the\n70\t   strategy author before scheduling.\n71\t2. **Epic size over the ~2-week guideline (managed).** E001 is estimated at\n72\t   ~1 sprint (the audit enumerates the exact defects per flow; remediation is\n73\t   mechanical focus-trap/reachability/indicator work across four flows in one\n74\t   codebase). Kept whole because the four flows are one logical change owned by one\n75\t   team in one repo, and a per-flow split fragments a single mechanical remediation\n76\t   pass while duplicating the shared focus-management and focus-indicator baseline\n77\t   across epics. Re-split trigger: the audit defect list for any single flow\n78\t   exceeds ~10 defects, or total remediation tracking passes the 2-week guideline —\n79\t   at that point split per flow, with each flow epic still carrying the shared\n80\t   focus-indicator consistency requirement.\n81\t3. **Possible priority inversion (non-blocking).** HLR-3 is P2, yet the problem\n82\t   statement says two public-sector customers require the published VPAT before\n83\t   renewal — a commercially gating deliverable. Per the docs-authoring priority\n84\t   exception the epic is P2 (strategy frontmatter priority: Normal), but scheduling\n85\t   should account for the renewal deadline; the fix work (E001) is correctly P1 and\n86\t   is on the critical path to the VPAT regardless.\n87\t4. **Component mapping note.** \"Documentation\" has no architecture-context file but\n88\t   is an existing canonical component in `.context/rhai-components.txt` (a docs\n89\t   content process, not a code component), so no repo/konflux onboarding chain was\n90\t   generated for it. Both epics' `component` values are verbatim canonical names;\n91\t   no non-obvious mappings were needed.\n92\t\n93\t## Tiered Delivery\n94\t\n95\tNot applicable — no external dependencies with uncertain timing; the strategy\n96\tdeclares none and the work is entirely in-platform.\n97\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-bd7d250278ca6a6a","agent